      idea: "This chapter is a puzzle, and Roy never spells out the answer. He writes in the first person as someone else: a local man on a freezing December Thursday after three days of poor fishing, not sure whether to fish at all. Two strange brothers are still camped on the beach, and everyone assumes they're a little crazy. A boy, little Johnny Ward, tags along. The brothers offer coffee; when Johnny asks where they got so many eggs, one brother jokes that a small hen running around lays eight to ten a day, and Johnny runs off to find the miracle hen.\n\nThe brothers say they're building a machine that will fly like a bird. The narrator politely changes the subject to Woolworth's plan for the tallest building in the world (the quiet brother's dry answer: that's a lot of nickels and dimes). Then the quiet brother sets up a box on three legs and asks if the narrator can make a photograph. He's never done it, he says, but he's willing to try. He squeezes the bulb as the noisy cloth-and-wire contraption passes, and the next thing he knows he's sitting on the ground with a crowd shouting, “Did you get it?”\n\nOnly at the end do the names arrive: Orville and Wilbur Wright, and the narrator is **John T. Daniels**, who has just taken the most famous photograph in the world, the first moment of the first powered flight. He's glad he didn't go fishing. And Johnny Ward comes back to announce that the hen is just a common-looking chicken. He chased a fake miracle and missed the real one (though Roy adds he'll live to watch a man walk on the moon through a “magic window,” television).\n\nThe hidden lessons, stated plainly:\n\n- **History looks ordinary while it's happening.** The locals saw two cranks freezing on a beach.\n- **Show up.** Daniels nearly went fishing. Being there put him in history.\n- **Say yes to the unfamiliar task.** “I've never done it, but I'm willing to try” is why his name lasts.\n- **Don't chase the miracle hen.** The boy ran after a joke and missed the flight in front of him.\n- **Someone has to squeeze the bulb.** The moment lasts because a person captured it.\n\nIt connects to Chapter 71, where the leaders of 1886 were blind to cars, catalogs and Coca-Cola being born around them, and to Chapter 70, which listed bicycle mechanic Wilbur Wright among the “nuclear visionaries.” The next chapter goes behind the scenes of this same flight.",
