      title: "Listening to Ted: everyone has something to teach you, if you don't laugh",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book", "Relationships"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 1998)", section: "Chapter 79 · Listening to Ted · pp. 168–169", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Schoolboy, every living person has something he can teach you.", page: "169" },
        { text: "you'll never find a true friend unless you're willing to be one.", page: "169" }
      ],
      idea: "At fifteen, Roy worked for Ted, a foul-mouthed, hard-bitten war veteran who ran a steel shop and called him “Schoolboy.” Roy got every job no real steelworker would touch: scrubbing the bathroom, mowing the lawn, hauling scrap to the junkyard. His favorite was hauling a thousand gallons of water to Ted's house whenever Ted's well ran dry.\n\nThe first time, they drove far out of town to a run-down gas station in the middle of nowhere. Ted walked behind a junk pile, pulled out a huge canvas hose, opened a hidden valve, and clean, bright water came gushing into their tank. On the silent ride home, Ted suddenly swerved into the left lane for no visible reason. When Roy finally asked, Ted pointed back at a big elm with a dead branch hanging over the road: someday it was going to fall, and Ted didn't want to be under it when it did.\n\nHere's the hinge of the story. Roy could have laughed. Instead he answered as seriously as he knew how: thanks for pointing that out, I'll try to remember it. Ted looked at him as if noticing him for the first time, and the wisdom came out. Every living person has something to teach you; find out what it is and let them teach you, because people are glad to teach valuable things if you'll let them. Then, after long pauses, two lessons on friendship: you only get a true friend by being one first, and a true friend stays beside you even when staying is expensive.\n\nRoy spells out the meaning in the last lines: Ted had trusted him with a private fear, Roy had taken it seriously instead of laughing, and **a valve opened**. The water hidden under the shabbiest gravel lot is the chapter's metaphor: the roughest-looking people often sit on the deepest wells, and the valve is respect, especially respect for the fear that sounds silly.\n\nThree lessons:\n\n- **Everyone is a teacher** if you go looking for the lesson.\n- **Taking someone's fear seriously earns trust.** Laughing would have closed the valve for good.\n- **Friendship is a two-way commitment** that shows itself when it costs something.\n\nThe next chapter flips the roles: a kind older man, Dick Bailey, pours encouragement into nineteen-year-old Roy.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Every living person has something to teach you. Your job is to find out what, and let them.",
        "Taking a person's private fear seriously opens a valve; laughing at it closes it.",
        "Treasure often sits under the shabbiest gravel lot: don't judge the well by the parking lot.",
        "You only find a true friend by being one, and a true friend doesn't back away when it costs him."
      ],
      why: "The best ad copy and the best client relationships come from listening to people others dismiss (the crusty owner, the nervous student, the office manager) and treating their “silly” fears as data. That's where the hidden water is.",
