      title: "Thinking with Your Heart: intuition wins where intellect only avoids losing",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book", "Decision-making"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 1998)", section: "Chapter 86 · Thinking with Your Heart · p. 182", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Intellect may not fail as often as intuition, but it doesn't win as often, either.", page: "182" },
        { text: "Sometimes the thing that makes the least sense is exactly the right thing to do.", page: "182" }
      ],
      idea: "This one-page chapter defines intuition and defends it. Intuition, Roy says, is thinking with your heart instead of your head: **getting to the correct answer without having all the facts**. It is, he says, the thing that makes people smarter than machines.\n\nHe draws the contrast in a memorable image. **Intellect is linear**: it puts facts in columns and rows. **Intuition is nonlinear**: it dumps all the facts into a big bowl, stirs them like soup, and watches to see what connects. Because intuition almost never arrives with proof, intuitive people struggle to persuade analytical ones; often they can't even explain how they know.\n\nThen his key claim (in the quotes): intellect makes fewer mistakes, but it also scores fewer victories. Leaders, explorers and inventors all rely on intuition, because it's what tells them how to go where nobody has been. There's no spreadsheet for a place no one has visited.\n\nRoy also says, in 1998 terms, that intuition is what makes women “smarter” (he's careful to say not “more intelligent”) than men, because men more often focus the mind and ignore the heart. Take the principle rather than the generalization: *anyone* who uses only the head is leaving half their intelligence unused.\n\nHe's careful not to trash intellect. It's to be cherished. He just wants intuition to get its due, and he wants you brave enough to act on your heart, because the choice that looks least logical is sometimes the right one.\n\nHow it fits the book: Chapter 58, *Intellect and Emotion*, said customers decide with emotion and then find logic to justify it, so the people you sell to are thinking with their hearts too. Chapter 87, next, shows what intellect does when it runs unchecked: Wile E. Coyote's overplanning. And Chapter 63, *Experiment!*, gives you the bridge for business. Let intuition choose the bet, then let a cheap test tell you whether the soup really connected. In advertising that's nearly free: you can run a gut-feel creative next to the logical one and let the market vote.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Intuition is reaching the right conclusion without enough information: thinking with your heart.",
        "Intellect is linear (columns and rows); intuition is nonlinear (stir everything in a bowl and watch what connects).",
        "Intellect fails less often but wins less often; leaders, explorers and inventors rely on intuition.",
        "Intuition rarely comes with proof, so it takes courage to follow.",
        "In business, pair them: intuition picks the bet, a cheap experiment checks it."
      ],
      why: "Many of ZDub's biggest calls (which hook, which client to take, which niche to chase) have to be made before the data exists. Zach needs the courage to trust his gut and the discipline to test it cheaply.",
      example: {
        title: "The gut-feel creative",
        before: "Data-only thinking: “Carousel ads with class prices and dates have the lowest cost per click in the PMT account, so make more of those.”",
        after: "Intuition: “Every student I talk to lights up describing the first time they rode a full lap without stalling. I think a 15-second clip of that moment, with no prices at all, will beat everything.” Zach runs it next to the best carousel at $20/day each for a week. Either way, he learns something.",
        body: "Intuition supplied an idea no spreadsheet would have suggested; a small experiment keeps it honest. Roy's point holds: the least logical-looking option sometimes wins, and you only find out by trying it."
      },
